WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.401
-No, no, no!

00:00:02.401 --> 00:00:04.604
[ Clattering ]

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:09.075
[ Breathing heavily ]

00:00:11.176 --> 00:00:13.480
[ Car engine starts ]

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:22.622 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:23.756
-Hey.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:25.125
[ Electricity crackles ]

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:27.827
-What the hell?
-Get out of the car!

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:28.928
-No.

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:30.163
-Get out of the [bleep] car!

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:32.098
-Wait, wait, wait.
Please, no, no, no.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.266 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:34.500
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.569
We're here with Aubrey Plaza,
everybody.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:37.903
[ Applause ]

00:00:37.903 --> 00:00:40.839
So this is a fantastic film.
-Thank you.

00:00:40.839 --> 00:00:42.207
-"Emily the Criminal."

00:00:42.207 --> 00:00:44.643
So this is a script that just
sort of came your way,

00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:46.813
and did you know right away
that you wanted to make it?

00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:48.181
-Yes.

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.250
It's an incredible script,
written by John Patton Ford,

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.784
who also directs the movie.

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:55.287
A colleague of mine who I was
working on something else with

00:00:55.287 --> 00:00:57.656
handed it to me
and was just like,

00:00:57.656 --> 00:00:58.757
"I think you'd
like the character,"

00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:00.459
and I read it so fast,

00:01:00.459 --> 00:01:03.329
and it's just like,
an undeniably good script,

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.630
and I was like,
"I gotta make this."

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:05.665
-It's a really --

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.299
It immediately touches on
the ideas

00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:10.303
of, you know, student loan debt
and the gig economy

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:13.139
and sort of the very small
decisions we can make

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:15.375
that sort of snowball into

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:18.144
becoming a member of
the sort of criminal underworld.

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.612
-Yeah.
-And you're so believable.

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.081
It feels like one of these
great, old '70s crime films.

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:24.150
It just has this real
propulsion behind it.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.051
-I know.
That's what I love about it.

00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:29.722
It's like, a real down and dirty
L.A. story.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:33.225
But the L.A. that you don't
get to see a lot in movies.

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:36.396
Like, mostly it's hipster
Silver Lake L.A. or whatever.

00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:38.131
But yeah,
it reminded me of, like,

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:40.233
"Straight Time"
with Dustin Hoffman.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:41.467
-Yeah.
-Or Michael Mann movies.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:42.635
I don't know.

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:43.803
There's just something
kind of gritty about it.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:46.106
-I know you shot it on
a very small budget,

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.074
but there's some great
action sequences,

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:49.275
great car chase scenes.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.144
Were those fun pulling off
on a shoestring?

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:53.412
-Yeah, I mean, they were fun --
very dangerous,

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:56.582
because we didn't have
a lot of money, so, you know.

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:58.184
[ Laughter ]
It was like, you know,

00:01:58.184 --> 00:01:59.852
certain streets
weren't locked down.

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:05.024
Day one, actually, when I
got in the car, I was like --

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.893
the camera was
in the front seat with me,

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:10.163
the DP was operating the camera,
the director's in the back,

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:11.397
just the three of us in the car.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.999
And I was like,
"So, where are we going?

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:14.900
Should I take a stroll
around the block, or...?"

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:16.435
And they were like,
"We're getting on the 10."

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:18.071
And I was like, "The 10?"

00:02:18.071 --> 00:02:19.805
Which is a highway
in Los Angeles

00:02:19.805 --> 00:02:21.073
that is really scary.

00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:22.875
Within, like, 10 minutes,

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:24.944
I was going 80 miles an hour
down the 10,

00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:26.979
getting in actual
road rage incidents with people.

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:28.714
-[ Laughs ] Great.

00:02:28.714 --> 00:02:30.650
-It all felt really real!

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.218
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:33.753
Which was not safe.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.488
But that's what you gotta do,
you know?

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:36.756
When you're making
independent films,

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.057
you gotta just
[bleep] go for it.

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:39.425
-Yeah, well, you went for it

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:40.426
and you could
see it on the screen.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:41.994
You play a criminal in this.

00:02:41.994 --> 00:02:44.263
There's a lot of stealing.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:45.431
Are you someone --

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:47.166
have you ever in real life
done any theft?

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:49.969
-No, I'm a good Catholic girl,
no.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.737
Uh, no, no, I'm not a stealer.

00:02:51.737 --> 00:02:53.639
I'm more of, like, a vandalizer.
-Oh, really?

00:02:53.639 --> 00:02:55.108
-Yeah.
-Did you vandalize

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:56.609
back in the day?

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:57.810
-Yeah, um...

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.513
but I was kind of like
a pathetic vandalizer.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:04.216
I would vandalize buildings
that were already abandoned

00:03:04.216 --> 00:03:07.620
and stuff, so it was more like
I was trying to be a punk.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.021
Like, I'd throw
bricks in a window

00:03:09.021 --> 00:03:11.291
but the window was
already broken, kind of thing.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:15.361
But there was one place that
I always wanted to vandalize,

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.396
and I still have it,
and even to this day,

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:20.233
every Christmas when I go home,
I drive by it.

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:22.301
It's a...
I don't know what it is.

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:25.637
Some kind of community center
called The Blue Ball Barn.

00:03:25.637 --> 00:03:26.972
-Uh-huh.
-And I have this fantasy

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:30.242
of putting an S on it,
and I've never done it.

00:03:30.242 --> 00:03:34.013
-So not on the "ball,"
I would imagine. The S?

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.481
-Blue Balls Barn.
-Yeah.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:36.682
You want it to say
"Blue Balls Barn."

00:03:36.682 --> 00:03:38.618
-I want it to say
"Blue Balls Barn."

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:40.453
[ Laughter ]
-For a community center.

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:42.121
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:43.756
-It's funny!
-It is funny.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:44.890
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.226
-I'm gonna do it.
-Do it at Christmas.

00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:49.695
-I almost did it, like,
two years ago.

00:03:49.695 --> 00:03:50.796
-Really?
-And I was like,

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:52.031
"I can't do this as an adult."
-No.

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:53.633
-If I get caught now...
-I know.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.568
-But any press is good press,
right?

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:57.236
That's what they say.
-Exactly.

00:03:57.236 --> 00:03:59.839
-For the Golden Globe
award push,

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.874
it would be great to
get arrested in Delaware.

00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:03.409
-You're right. You're right.

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:04.477
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:06.779
-Hey, congratulations on
everything.

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:07.846
-Thanks, Seth!

00:04:07.846 --> 00:04:08.915
-Thank you so much for
being here.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.149
You guys, Aubrey Plaza.
-Thank you.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.085
"Emily the Criminal" is
streaming now on Netflix,

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:14.520
and "The White Lotus"
is streaming on HBO Max.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.654
We'll be right back

00:04:15.654 --> 00:04:17.724
with Bashir Salahuddin
and Diallo Riddle.

